## The organ nobody wants to talk about

People discuss sore knees over coffee and blood pressure over lunch. That they feel bloated after every meal, that they have avoided anything fatty for years, that a gallbladder now dictates the weekly shopping — that tends to stay unsaid until a consultation. Yet **spa treatment for digestive disorders** is one of the oldest disciplines Mariánské Lázně has. The water here was famous before the first hotel was built: salty, heavy, strongly mineralised, and it did exactly what was expected of it.

Late summer and autumn suit this kind of stay. Three months of irregular meals, holidays and unfamiliar cooking leave their mark on almost everyone; after the main season the town quietens and the queues at the springs grow shorter. Above all, a drinking cure is largely a matter of walking — and September and October are far better for walking than August.

Getting here is straightforward. Prague is roughly 160 kilometres away, and the town sits on the main line between Prague and Cheb, so the journey works perfectly well without a car.

## Why this town in particular

Mariánské Lázně lies at around 630 metres above sea level on volcanic bedrock. More than forty cold mineral springs surface within the town itself and roughly a hundred more in the surrounding countryside. They are cold acidulous waters at a steady 7 to 10 °C all year, so their composition does not shift with the season.

The flagship of the digestive indications is the **Cross Spring** (Křížový pramen). According to the town's own figures it is a sulphate-bicarbonate-sodium ferrous acidulous water with a total mineralisation of **9,845 mg/l**, a sulphate content of **3,130 mg/l**, **2,700 mg/l** of sodium and **2,305 mg/l** of free carbon dioxide. That combination of sulphate and sodium is what is commonly called Glauber's salt — and it is the source of the water's well-known laxative effect. The Cross Spring pavilion on the colonnade is open daily from 6 a.m. to 6 p.m.

Other springs play their part too. The **Forest Spring** (Lesní pramen) is traditionally associated with disorders of the upper respiratory tract and the digestive system, while the **Ferdinand Spring** is close to the Cross Spring in composition and features in metabolic and digestive prescriptions. Which spring, in what quantity and in what order is not a matter of taste but of a written medical plan. A full overview of the springs and their properties is on the [springs overview](/en/springs-overview) page.

{% spring-cards indication="digestion" /%}

## What the indication list actually says

Czech spa medicine works from a binding indication list — decree No. 2/2015 Coll., amended several times since, most recently by decree No. 586/2025 Coll. Diseases of the digestive system form **indication group III**, and Mariánské Lázně is a listed spa town for it. In the local spa operator's own version of the list, the group is broken down into eight positions (III/1 to III/8); the English edition for self-paying guests lists the individual diagnoses as follows:

{% indication-picker group="digestive" /%}

The list also sets length of stay and who pays — although the payment rules apply to people insured within the Czech public system. So-called **comprehensive spa care** (marked K, 21 or 28 days) is covered there including accommodation and meals; under **contributory care** (P, 14 or 21 days) only the treatment itself is covered.

For international visitors the list is useful for a different reason. It is a precise statement of which diagnoses are treated here under medical supervision and how long a meaningful stay lasts. Most guests from outside the Czech Republic arrange the stay privately, which in practice means the same programme without the paperwork.

