## A drinking cure is not a drinks machine

{% figure src="/images/library/drinking-cure/spring-drinking-cure.jpg" alt="Older man cupping his hands to drink mineral water from an ornate bronze spring spout outdoors." caption="The water is drunk right at the spring, slowly and in small sips. How much, and from which spring, is set out in the doctor's plan." /%}

This is where most of the misunderstanding starts. A drinking cure looks simple — cup, spring, walk — which is exactly why advice such as "drink three litres a day" circulates so freely. With the Mariánské Lázně springs that advice is at best pointless and at worst harmful.

Water from the Cross Spring is strongly mineralised and, thanks to its sulphates, acts as a laxative. **The volume, the choice of spring, the temperature of the water and the timing relative to meals are always part of a medical prescription**, drawn up after the initial examination and adjusted during the stay. Someone with gallstones is given a different plan from someone recovering from gastric resection, and different again from a patient with chronic pancreatitis. There is no universal daily quantity — and anyone offering you one is not describing treatment.

The rest of the method is unglamorous and effective: drink slowly, in small sips, at the prescribed interval before meals, and while walking. Walking between sips is not folklore but part of the procedure — gentle movement supports transit and is also the most natural form of the light physical activity recommended for digestive conditions. The full method is set out in the [drinking cure guide](/en/magazine/drinking-cure-guide).

{% cure-path /%}

## What the evidence supports

Honesty first: balneology has its strongest evidence in musculoskeletal medicine, not in digestion. An umbrella review published in 2021, summarising 49 systematic reviews on the clinical efficacy of medical hydrology, concluded that the benefit is best documented for certain musculoskeletal conditions, chronic venous insufficiency and inflammatory skin diseases — while describing the evidence for inhalation and drinking therapies as "more limited, although interesting" ([Antonelli et al., 2021](https://doi.org/10.1007/s00484-021-02133-w), *International Journal of Biometeorology*).

That does not mean drinking cures do not work. It means less is known about them than they deserve. One of the few large randomised trials looked at a sulphate- and magnesium-rich natural mineral water in functional constipation: in a double-blind study of 226 outpatients, 50 % responded to treatment by day 14 compared with 29 % in the control group, with a mean time to response of 6.4 days ([Dupont et al., 2019](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nut.2019.02.018), *Nutrition*). That was a French water of different composition, and the study was funded by its producer, so it proves nothing about the Cross Spring. What it does show is the principle: sulphate waters have a measurable, not merely subjective, effect on the bowel. Both papers can be found through the PubMed database.

The practical conclusion is a sober one. A spa cure replaces neither gastroenterology nor medication. The aim is calm, a settled daily rhythm, a diet that has been tested on you, and three weeks in which the body has a chance to step out of chronic irritation.

## What a day looks like

{% figure src="/images/library/colonnade/colonnade-interior-hall.jpg" alt="Long cast-iron colonnade hall with painted vaulted ceiling, sunlit floor patterns and shops along one side." caption="The day begins on the colonnade: the morning cup, a slow walk, and only then breakfast." /%}

The rhythm is deliberately monotonous. That is the point.

The morning begins at the spring before breakfast, with a cup and a slow walk along the colonnade. Breakfast follows, according to the prescribed dietary regime. Mornings are given over to treatments: mineral baths, dry carbon dioxide baths, procedures that improve circulation, and for some diagnoses electrotherapy or magnetotherapy. The dry carbon dioxide baths use natural Maria's gas, 99.7 % carbon dioxide — a resource the Ensana spa houses have in the town itself and which does not occur anywhere else in that purity. After every treatment comes rest, lying down; it is part of the therapy rather than a courtesy.

Afternoons belong to movement — the light activity that supports digestion, usually walking in the spa forests. The paths start a few hundred metres beyond the colonnade and climb into the Slavkov Forest; you choose the gradient by fitness, not ambition. Evenings are quiet: another cup as prescribed, and an early night.

Once a week there is a consultation at which the drinking plan and the treatment schedule are adjusted. That weekly feedback is precisely what outpatient care cannot offer — instead of ten minutes once a quarter, a continuous picture emerges.

{% figure src="/images/library/town/aerial-spa-quarter.jpg" alt="Elevated view of spa-quarter buildings set against a forested hillside, with a green lawn in the foreground." caption="Spa houses beneath the wooded hillside. Forest paths start a few hundred metres from the colonnade, and the afternoon walk is part of the treatment." /%}

## A diet that is not a punishment

{% figure src="/images/library/cuisine/green-salad-tomatoes-basil.jpg" alt="A composed salad of frisée lettuce, cherry tomatoes, enoki mushrooms, basil and sprouts in a white bowl." caption="The diet is not a punishment. For three weeks someone cooks to your diagnosis for you, and you find out how much you can actually eat." /%}

For digestive indications the individually designed dietary programme matters as much as the water. And it has one decisive advantage over attempts made at home: for three weeks somebody else does the cooking.

That is the real trick. People who have lived with restrictions for years usually know exactly what they must avoid and rarely what they may enjoy. The spa kitchen demonstrates what a menu looks like when it respects the diagnosis and still tastes of something. That experience is often the one thing guests take home for years, because treatments end but cooking carries on.

## When a stay is not appropriate

For every group the indication list also sets out contraindications and admission requirements — which examinations are needed, how recent they must be, and which specialist may recommend the stay. For digestive diagnoses this usually means a current gastroenterological report, and for post-operative conditions an endoscopic confirmation.

That assessment belongs to your own doctor or gastroenterologist, not to the spa. Without medical documentation the spa physician here cannot build the programme that makes the journey worthwhile. A general overview is on the [indications and contraindications](/en/indications-and-contraindications) page.

## Why autumn works

The classic cure lasts three weeks and is repeated for chronic diagnoses — the [guide to the three-week cure](/en/magazine/classic-three-week-cure-marienbad) describes how it unfolds. If your dates fall in September or October, several advantages arrive at once.

After the main season the town settles, waits at the springs are shorter and treatments are easier to schedule. The weather suits long walks between sips far better than high summer does. And the town is still alive: the international folklore festival Mariánský podzim runs from 17 to 20 September 2026 at the colonnade and the municipal theatre, and the Singing Fountain plays until 31 October, when it closes the season. Further arguments for this time of year are collected on the page about [autumn in Mariánské Lázně](/en/autumn).

Bring a spa cup or buy one on the colonnade, sturdy shoes for the forest paths, clothes you can layer — and above all a current medical report. The rest is handled by three weeks of regularity and by a water that has run here unchanged for more than two hundred years.
